2026-06-30 15:51:37,898 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e1603f4d-4a99-4a0c-9ac5-532904bea7b3', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF factor value terms. Factor values identify which metadata\nattribute is the primary experimental variable being compared in the study.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: factor value cell line\nsdrf_column: factor value[cell line]\nontology: CLO:0000031\ndescription: Indicates cell line is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return a value if different cell lines are compared across conditions.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value compound\nsdrf_column: factor value[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:37577\ndescription: Indicates compound/drug treatment is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return the compound name if different compounds or drug doses are\n  compared. Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value disease\nsdrf_column: factor value[disease]\nontology: OGMS:0000031, MONDO, EFO, DOID\ndescription: Indicates disease state is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return disease name/accession if diseased vs. normal or different\n  disease states are compared. Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value dose\nsdrf_column: factor value[dose]\nontology: EFO:0000428\ndescription: Indicates dose/concentration is the primary experimental variable.\npattern: number + unit\ninstructions: Return the dose range if different doses of a compound are compared.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value organism part\nsdrf_column: factor value[organism part]\nontology: EFO:0000635, UBERON, BTO\ndescription: Indicates tissue/organ is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return the tissue/organ name if different tissues are compared.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value time\nsdrf_column: factor value[time]\nontology: EFO:0000721\ndescription: Indicates time point is the primary experimental variable.\npattern: number + time unit\ninstructions: Return the time points compared if a time-course experiment.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value treatment\nsdrf_column: factor value[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Indicates treatment/perturbation is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return the treatment type if different treatments are compared\n  (e.g. different growth conditions, carbon sources, stress conditions).\n  Otherwise return null.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string or null — null if this variable is not\n           the primary experimental factor in this study)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Abstract", "Methods"), or null\n\nNote: Most XL-MS datasets will have only one or two non-null factor values.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nA Proximity dependent labeling approach to define flotillin-2 interacting proteins within Membrane Domains of HeLa cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe formation of plasma membrane microdomains, known as lipid rafts, is driven by specific interactions among cholesterol, sphingolipids, and certain proteins such as flotillins. Flotillins are known to oligomerize and associate with defined lipid assemblies, thereby promoting the coalescence and stabilization of these membrane rafts. Acting as scaffolding molecules, flotillins play critical roles in various cellular processes, including the spatial organization of signaling complexes and membrane compartmentalization. To identify flotillin-associated proteins within membrane rafts in vivo, we employed the BioID proximity labeling technique to analyze the flotillin-2 interactome in living HeLa cells. Flotillin-2 was fused to the promiscuous biotin ligase BirA* at either the N- or C-terminus and expressed in HeLa cells, enabling biotinylation of proximal proteins. Biotinylated proteins were enriched on streptavidin affinity beads and subsequently identified via LC-MS analysis. This investigation of the flotillin-2 interactome provides new insights into the molecular composition of membrane rafts and offers a valuable framework for understanding the mechanisms underlying raft formation, regulation, and associated cellular functions in native membranes.\n\n[METHODS]\nPlasmid constructs were generated using the pSTVH8 vector encoding flotillin-2 fused to the promiscuous biotin ligase BirA* at either the N- or C-terminus. As controls, constructs encoding monomeric enhanced GFP (mEGFP) fused to BirA* at both termini were similarly prepared. These plasmids were transiently transfected into HeLa cells to enable inducible expression of BirA*-tagged flotillin-2. 48 hours post-transfection, biotin was added to the culture medium to facilitate biotinylation of endogenous proteins in close proximity to the BirA*-tagged flotillin-2. To enrich for membrane domain associated proteins, detergent-resistant membrane (DRM) fractions were isolated by solubilizing cellular membranes with a non-ionic detergent followed by sucrose density gradient centrifugation. DRMs were then subjected to streptavidin-based affinity purification. After extensive washing (including three last washes in a detergent-free buffer) bead-bound proteins were denatured at 65°C for 10 minutes in 25 mM Tris pH 7.5, 0.1% sodium deoxycholate. Subsequently, 100 ng of trypsin was added to the samples for an overnight on-bead digestion in 37°C. Next day the solution was separated from the beads, acidified and sodium deoxycholate was removed by centrifugation. The supernatant was then desalted with a STAGE tip procedure. LC-MS was performed on an M-Class Acquity UPLC connected to a Synapt XS HDMS equipped with a nanoESI source. Mobile phase A consisted of H2O + 0.1% FA, while mobile phase B of ACN + 0.1% FA. An 8–40% B 60min linear gradient at a 300nL/min flow rate was applied for sample separation on a C18 75μm x 250mm analytical column kept at 45°C. A 5-minute sample trapping step was performed prior to analytical column separation. MS data were collected in ion mobility DIA (HDMSE) at a scan rate of 0.6 seconds in a 50–2000 m/z range. Source conditions were fine-tuned as follows: Capillary: 2.6kV; Sampling Cone: 35V; Source Offset: 15V; Source temperature: 80°C; cone gas flow: 40L/h, purge gas flow: 100L/h, NanoFlow gas pressure: 0.5Bar. For MS2, a collision energy ramp of 27–47V was set on the transfer cell. A (Glu1)-Fibrinopeptide B solution was acquired in-parallel in reference function, and mass correction was applied post-acquisition.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
