2026-06-30 15:51:45,131 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-4c5b0b8a-30a1-4d18-9378-1c94b37ab430', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\nFor each term, return the best available ontology-resolved value with its accession where indicated.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: organism\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism]\nontology: NCBITaxon (e.g. NCBITaxon:9606)\ndescription: Source species of the proteome of interest in the biological samples analyzed by mass spectrometry. Return the scientific name. \nSource species maybe different than host species when proteins from one organism are expressed in another. \ninstructions: Use NCBITaxon accession. Common examples: Homo sapiens (NCBITaxon:9606),\n  Mus musculus (NCBITaxon:10090), Saccharomyces cerevisiae (NCBITaxon:4932),\n  Escherichia coli (NCBITaxon:562). If multiple species, return a list.\n\nterm: organism part\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism part]\nontology: UBERON or BTO\ndescription: Main normalized anatomical term for the sample analyzed by mass spectrometry. \ninstructions: Use the clearest ontology-backed anatomy term. Examples: whole-cell lysate, mitochondria,\n  nucleus, cytoplasm, liver, heart, brain, cell, ect. Return null if not applicable\n  (e.g. purified recombinant proteins with no tissue origin).\n\nterm: cell line\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell line]\nontology: CLO, BTO, or EFO (e.g. CLO:0001230 for HEK293)\ndescription: Name of the cell line if a cell line was used.\ninstructions: Return null if no cell line was used (primary cells or tissues).\n\nterm: cell type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell type]\nontology: CL, BTO, or CLO\ndescription: Cell type of the sample.\ninstructions: Return null if not applicable or not stated.\n\nterm: material type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[material type]\nontology: PRIDE:0000837\ndescription: Type of biological material analyzed. Differentiates whether the whole cell lysate was analyzed, just a specific anatomical structure or tissue, or if the samples were purified for specific proteins before mass spectrometry analysis. \nallowed_values: tissue, whole cell lysate, organell, membrane, synthetic, purified proteins, affinity captured\ninstructions: Choose the best matching fixed value. Return null if unclear.\n\nterm: strain or breed\nsdrf_column: characteristics[strain or breed]\nontology: free text\ndescription: Strain, breed, cultivar, or ecotype of the organism.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: developmental stage\nsdrf_column: characteristics[developmental stage]\nontology: EFO\ndescription: Developmental stage of the organism or donor.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: technology type\nsdrf_column: technology type\nontology: PRIDE:0000663\ndescription: Type of technology used. For XL-MS this is always fixed.\nallowed_values: proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry" for XL-MS datasets.\n\nterm: source name\nsdrf_column: source name\nontology: PRIDE:0000623\ndescription: Unique identifier for the biological sample. Typically derived from\n  the dataset file names or sample descriptions in the paper.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention used (e.g. "PXD_stem" or "condition_replicate").\n  This is auto-generated from file names; return a description of the sample naming pattern only.\n\nterm: assay name\nsdrf_column: assay name\nontology: NCIT:C178857\ndescription: Unique identifier for each MS run/assay. Typically the raw file stem.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention. This is auto-generated; return the pattern only.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable (e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"), else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text supporting the extraction, or null\n  "evidence_location": where in the paper (e.g. "Methods", "Abstract"), or null\n\nExample output structure:\n{\n  "characteristics[organism]": {"value": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae", "accession": "NCBITaxon:4932", "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  "characteristics[organism part]": {"value": "mitochondria", "accession": null, "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  ...\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nA Proximity dependent labeling approach to define flotillin-2 interacting proteins within Membrane Domains of HeLa cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe formation of plasma membrane microdomains, known as lipid rafts, is driven by specific interactions among cholesterol, sphingolipids, and certain proteins such as flotillins. Flotillins are known to oligomerize and associate with defined lipid assemblies, thereby promoting the coalescence and stabilization of these membrane rafts. Acting as scaffolding molecules, flotillins play critical roles in various cellular processes, including the spatial organization of signaling complexes and membrane compartmentalization. To identify flotillin-associated proteins within membrane rafts in vivo, we employed the BioID proximity labeling technique to analyze the flotillin-2 interactome in living HeLa cells. Flotillin-2 was fused to the promiscuous biotin ligase BirA* at either the N- or C-terminus and expressed in HeLa cells, enabling biotinylation of proximal proteins. Biotinylated proteins were enriched on streptavidin affinity beads and subsequently identified via LC-MS analysis. This investigation of the flotillin-2 interactome provides new insights into the molecular composition of membrane rafts and offers a valuable framework for understanding the mechanisms underlying raft formation, regulation, and associated cellular functions in native membranes.\n\n[METHODS]\nPlasmid constructs were generated using the pSTVH8 vector encoding flotillin-2 fused to the promiscuous biotin ligase BirA* at either the N- or C-terminus. As controls, constructs encoding monomeric enhanced GFP (mEGFP) fused to BirA* at both termini were similarly prepared. These plasmids were transiently transfected into HeLa cells to enable inducible expression of BirA*-tagged flotillin-2. 48 hours post-transfection, biotin was added to the culture medium to facilitate biotinylation of endogenous proteins in close proximity to the BirA*-tagged flotillin-2. To enrich for membrane domain associated proteins, detergent-resistant membrane (DRM) fractions were isolated by solubilizing cellular membranes with a non-ionic detergent followed by sucrose density gradient centrifugation. DRMs were then subjected to streptavidin-based affinity purification. After extensive washing (including three last washes in a detergent-free buffer) bead-bound proteins were denatured at 65°C for 10 minutes in 25 mM Tris pH 7.5, 0.1% sodium deoxycholate. Subsequently, 100 ng of trypsin was added to the samples for an overnight on-bead digestion in 37°C. Next day the solution was separated from the beads, acidified and sodium deoxycholate was removed by centrifugation. The supernatant was then desalted with a STAGE tip procedure. LC-MS was performed on an M-Class Acquity UPLC connected to a Synapt XS HDMS equipped with a nanoESI source. Mobile phase A consisted of H2O + 0.1% FA, while mobile phase B of ACN + 0.1% FA. An 8–40% B 60min linear gradient at a 300nL/min flow rate was applied for sample separation on a C18 75μm x 250mm analytical column kept at 45°C. A 5-minute sample trapping step was performed prior to analytical column separation. MS data were collected in ion mobility DIA (HDMSE) at a scan rate of 0.6 seconds in a 50–2000 m/z range. Source conditions were fine-tuned as follows: Capillary: 2.6kV; Sampling Cone: 35V; Source Offset: 15V; Source temperature: 80°C; cone gas flow: 40L/h, purge gas flow: 100L/h, NanoFlow gas pressure: 0.5Bar. For MS2, a collision energy ramp of 27–47V was set on the transfer cell. A (Glu1)-Fibrinopeptide B solution was acquired in-parallel in reference function, and mass correction was applied post-acquisition.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
