 were then subjected to streptavidin-based affinity purification. After extensive washing (including three last washes in a detergent-free buffer) bead-bound proteins were denatured at 65°C for 10 minutes in 25 mM Tris pH 7.5, 0.1% sodium deoxycholate. Subsequently, 100 ng of trypsin was added to the samples for an overnight on-bead digestion in 37°C. Next day the solution was separated from the beads, acidified and sodium deoxycholate was removed by centrifugation. The supernatant was then desalted with a STAGE tip procedure. LC-MS was performed on an M-Class Acquity UPLC connected to a Synapt XS HDMS equipped with a nanoESI source. Mobile phase A consisted of H2O + 0.1% FA, while mobile phase B of ACN + 0.1% FA. An 8–40% B 60min linear gradient at a 300nL/min flow rate was applied for sample separation on a C18 75μm x 250mm analytical column kept at 45°C. A 5-minute sample trapping step was performed prior to analytical column separation. MS data were collected in ion mobility DIA (HDMSE) at a scan rate of 0.6 seconds in a 50–2000 m/z range. Source conditions were fine-tuned as follows: Capillary: 2.6kV; Sampling Cone: 35V; Source Offset: 15V; Source temperature: 80°C; cone gas flow: 40L/h, purge gas flow: 100L/h, NanoFlow gas pressure: 0.5Bar. For MS2, a collision energy ramp of 27–47V was set on the transfer cell. A (Glu1)-Fibrinopeptide B solution was acquired in-parallel in reference function, and mass correction was applied post-acquisition.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
